 ## Overview
-Splat-to-Voxel Transfer converts unstructured 3D Gaussian primitives into a structured, dense voxel grid by depositing splat attributes onto grid cells [S1]. It bridges point-based Gaussian representations with grid-based neural processing, enabling voxel transformers for feature fusion and refinement [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that focus on sparse grid generation or direct format conversion, this method explicitly constructs a coarse-to-fine hierarchy and applies geometric weight-based sparsification to reduce computational load for downstream transformer networks [S1].
+Splat-to-Voxel Transfer is a method for converting continuous 3D Gaussian primitives into a structured, discrete voxel grid representation. It solves the problem of aggregating point-based splat attributes (features and geometry) into a dense spatial proxy, enabling subsequent processing by voxel-based neural networks [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that may focus on direct voxel format generation or kernel interpolation without hierarchical culling, this method explicitly constructs a coarse-to-fine hierarchy and applies weight-based sparsification to reduce computational load for downstream transformers [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in three main phases: Splat Depositing, Splat Fusion, and Coarse-to-fine Hierarchy Construction with Sparsification.
-
-**Inputs:**
-- Set of Gaussian primitives $G_k$, each defined by mean position $\mu_k$, opacity $\alpha_k$, feature vector $F_k$, and other Gaussian parameters $G_k$ (e.g., covariance, spherical harmonics coefficients) [S1].
-- Target high-resolution voxel grid dimensions and resolution [S1].
-
-**Outputs:**
-- Sparse set of fine-level voxel attributes $E_j$ and corresponding refined Gaussian parameters $G'_j$ [S1].
-- Coarse-level voxel features $E^c_i$ used for transformer input [S1].
-
-**Data Structures:**
-- **Voxel Grid:** A 3D array where each voxel $V_j$ stores an attribute vector $E_j$ and a weight $W_j$ [S1].
-- **Coarse Grid:** A downsampled version of the fine grid, where each coarse voxel $V^c_i$ corresponds to a block $N_i$ of $d \times h \times w$ fine voxels [S1].
-- **Tokens:** Coarse voxel features reshaped into 1D vectors for transformer input [S1].
-
-**Parameters:**
-- **Nearest Voxels:** The implementation uses the adjacent 8 voxels for each splat [S1].
-- **Sparsification Ratio:** Top 20% of coarse voxels are retained [S1].
-- **Kernel Function:** $\mathcal{K}$ is a distance kernel (specific function details in supplementary material of [S1], not explicitly defined in the main text, but implied to be normalized) [S1].
-
